PPU 8 MiB allocation granularity for the opt-in MemPool line of torch_memory_saver.

Based on:
  PPU source   https://github.com/zhuzilin/torch_memory_saver @ bdab8c55
               (TMS_LINE=vmm, the default; installed as-is, this file does not
               apply to it)
  Relax patch  none -- docker/Dockerfile.cu13 pins the opt-in line's source,
               https://github.com/redai-studio/torch_memory_saver @ afc13785c5,
               unpatched, so this file is the whole delta there: PPU allocates
               in 8 MiB granules and cuMemCreate rejects any size that is not a
               multiple of it.

bdab8c55 keeps working with expandable_segments because it hooks cuMemCreate /
cuMemMap; the afc13785 line drives the saver through torch.cuda.MemPool and
refuses expandable_segments.

diff --git a/csrc/core.cpp b/csrc/core.cpp
index 914d18a..b0f81d8 100644
--- a/csrc/core.cpp
+++ b/csrc/core.cpp
@@ -36,6 +36,24 @@ cudaError_t TorchMemorySaver::malloc(void **ptr, CUdevice device, size_t size, c
     return ROCmHIPImplementation::rocm_malloc(ptr, device, size, tag, enable_cpu_backup, allocation_metadata_, allocator_metadata_mutex_);
 
 #else
+    // The minimum allocation granularity is platform specific (2 MiB on NVIDIA,
+    // 8 MiB on PPU) and cuMemCreate rejects anything that is not a multiple of
+    // it.  torch's caching allocator rounds large blocks to 2 MiB, so without
+    // this every allocation on a coarser platform fails with INVALID_VALUE.
+    // The aligned size is what metadata.size stores, and free/pause/resume all
+    // work off metadata.size, so the bookkeeping stays consistent.
+    {
+        CUmemAllocationProp alignment_prop = {};
+        alignment_prop.type = CU_MEM_ALLOCATION_TYPE_PINNED;
+        alignment_prop.location.type = CU_MEM_LOCATION_TYPE_DEVICE;
+        alignment_prop.location.id = device;
+        size_t granularity = 0;
+        CURESULT_CHECK(cuMemGetAllocationGranularity(&granularity, &alignment_prop, CU_MEM_ALLOC_GRANULARITY_MINIMUM));
+        if (granularity > 1 && size % granularity != 0) {
+            size = ((size + granularity - 1) / granularity) * granularity;
+        }
+    }
+
     const uint64_t memory_margin_bytes = memory_margin_bytes_.load();
     if (memory_margin_bytes > 0) {
         size_t free_bytes, total_bytes;
